  playing gameplay is important and all  but if i can't feel some sort of  attachment to what i'm actually doing  i quickly lose motivation obviously i've  already put out a video on the intro to  the story of which i'll link on the  screen now  but what i was trying to gauge from this  demo was how the story was going to be  told  the background leading up to this point  in the story is good although quite  generic  it locks onto a familiar aspect of what  we face in real life today  humanity has screwed up earth and is  forced to explore options  away from their dying home planet now  you may have that person in your life  that's always telling you to read the  book because it's so much better than  the movie  i was never that person i'd much prefer  to sit on my fat ass and have the tv  doing all the thinking for me  but over time i've come to the  realization that when it comes to most  video games that this is very much the  case  at least for me anyway when too many  cutscenes are given in a game this can  actually drive me away from the story  not just because in most games i feel  like they do a terrible job of it  but because it fills in the gaps that i  would have otherwise come up with on my  own  and in some cases i feel that my  imagination could have come up with a  far more interesting story  i'm not sure if this is actually making  any sense i could probably write a whole  series on this topic alone but long  story short  giving too much detail can actually  remove the immersion for me  the way the characters talk and react  can often be completely different to the  way that i would play out the situation  in my mind  making it difficult for me to relate  unpopular  opinion coming i actually prefer the  silent main character  that some games provide i'm certainly  not so  self-absorbed to think that this is the  way that everyone feels  much like myself with movies a lot if  not most  would prefer to have the story all laid  out in front of them  in 